PRJNA987743	Bos indicus	9915.0		Brazil	104	WGS	pubmed	38163076.0	38163076.0	Rumen and fecal microbiomes are related to diet and production traits in Bos indicus beef cattle.	Conteville LC	Regitano LCA	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Ruminants harbor a complex microbial community within their gastrointestinal tract, which plays major roles in their health and physiology. Brazil is one of the largest producers of beef in the world and more than 90% of the beef cattle herds are composed of pure and crossbred Nelore (Bos indicus). Despite its importance to the Brazilian economy and human feeding, few studies have characterized the Nelore microbiome. Therefore, using shotgun metagenomics, we investigated the impact of diet on the composition and functionality of the Nelore microbiome, and explored the associations between specific microbial taxa and their functionality with feed efficiency and methane emission. RESULTS: The ruminal microbiome exhibited significantly higher microbial diversity, distinctive taxonomic profile and variations in microbial functionality compared to the fecal microbiome, highlighting the distinct contributions of the microbiomes of these environments. Animals subjected to different dietary treatments exhibited significant differences in their microbiomes' archaeal diversity and in the abundance of 89 genera, as well as in the functions associated with the metabolism of components of each diet. Moreover, depending on the diet, feed-efficient animals and low methane emitters displayed higher microbial diversity in their fecal microbiome. Multiple genera were associated with an increase or decrease of the phenotypes. Upon analyzing the functions attributed to these taxa, we observed significant differences on the ruminal taxa associated with feed efficient and inefficient cattle. The ruminal taxa that characterized feed efficient cattle stood out for having significantly more functions related to carbohydrate metabolism, such as monosaccharides, di-/oligosaccharides and amino acids. The taxa associated with methane emission had functions associated with methanogenesis and the production of substrates that may influence methane production, such as hydrogen and formate. CONCLUSION: Our findings highlight the significant role of diet in shaping Nelore microbiomes and how its composition and functionality may affect production traits such as feed efficiency and methane emission. These insights provide valuable support for the implementation of novel feeding and biotechnological strategies.	2023.0	Frontiers in microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Bovidae	Bos	Bos indicus	10.3389/fmicb.2023.1282851				WGS:104	104	1	Bos indicus		gut metagenome	Feces of Animal 1460; Feces of Animal 1462; Feces of Animal 1464; Feces of Animal 1468; Feces of Animal 1476; Feces of Animal 1479; Feces of Animal 1480; Feces of Animal 1481; Feces of Animal 1485; Feces of Animal 1493; Feces of Animal 1494; Feces of Animal 1495; Feces of Animal 1496; Feces of Animal 1498; Feces of Animal 1500; Feces of Animal 1501; Feces of Animal 1502; Feces of Animal 1503; Feces of Animal 1504; Feces of Animal 238	1460_Feces; 1460_Rumen; 1462_Feces; 1462_Rumen; 1464_Feces	104.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2023.1282851	True	True	Bos indicus	True	Bos indicus beef cattle	True	Nelore (Bos indicus)	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on rumen and fecal samples collected from Nelore beef cattle.	UNIFORM	104	METAGENOMIC:104	104
PRJNA1206810	feces metagenome			China:jining	9	WGS	pubmed	40635073.0	40635073.0	The development and excretion of Toxoplasma gondii oocyst manipulate the gut microbiota in its definitive host.	Zhao GH	Yin K	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Oocysts serve as the primary source of Toxoplasma infection. Therefore, understanding oocyst development and exploring effective strategies to prevent oocyst excretion are crucial for controlling toxoplasmosis. METHODS: In this study, shotgun metagenomics was employed to characterize the functional and compositional changes in the gut microbiota of cats during oocyst development. The Spearman correlation test was utilized to analyze the correlation between differential Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) pathways and carbohydrate-active enzymes (CAZymes) in key bacteria regulating oocyst excretion. RESULTS: The results revealed that group A (sexual initiation stage) displayed a lower number of functional genes, which were restored to normal levels in group B (oocyst excretion stage), compared with group C (Toxoplasma-uninfected samples). The abundance of 39 KEGG pathways, 106 CAZymes, and 98 virulence factors (VFs) varied significantly among the three groups. The atrazine degradation pathway, associated with sexual development, was upregulated in group B. CAZymes involved in restoring the intestinal mucosal barrier and VFs related to iron metabolism, antibiotic resistance, and suppression of host immunity were enriched in group B. Sexual initiation and oocyst excretion resulted in reduced gut bacterial diversity and microbiota dysbiosis. Probiotics and bacteria related to linoleic acid (LA) uptake were dominant in both group A and group B. Bacteroides stercoris was the most significantly upregulated bacterium and could influence the expression of carbohydrate-binding modules (CBMs) and glycoside hydrolases (GHs) in group B. CONCLUSIONS: During the oocyst development/excretion stage, the function and composition of the cat gut microbiota changed significantly. In addition, Bacteroides stercoris may play a crucial role in oocyst excretion by regulating key candidates of CBMs and GHs. Our findings lay the foundation for investigating the regulatory mechanisms of oocyst excretion.	2025.0	Parasites & vectors	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Carnivora	Felidae	Felis	catus	10.1186/s13071-025-06925-6				WGS:9	9	0			feces metagenome	Laboratory grown cat feces 0d for C1; Laboratory grown cat feces 0d for C2; Laboratory grown cat feces 0d for C3; Laboratory grown cat feces 10d for B1; Laboratory grown cat feces 10d for B2; Laboratory grown cat feces 10d for B3; Laboratory grown cat feces 5d for A1; Laboratory grown cat feces 5d for A2; Laboratory grown cat feces 5d for A3	Metagenome or environmental sample from feces metagenome	9.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s13071-025-06925-6	True	False		True	cat	True	cat	False		False	paper only	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples from cats during different stages of Toxoplasma gondii oocyst development and excretion.	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:9	9
